\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}

\title{The first law for the layered cube}

\p{The layered model needs a reachability test. A tuple of permutations and
orientations can describe a dismantled cube without being obtainable by legal
slice turns. We now choose the labelled, fixed-frame state space from
[the layered-cube model](frap-0003) and state the first law for that space.
The theorem is a statement about labelled cubies; an ordinary colour pattern
is its observation after equal-looking labels have been forgotten.}

\subtree{
  \title{Coordinates for one state space}

  \p{Let #{K_o=(N-3)/2} for odd #{N} and #{K_e=N/2-1} for even #{N}.
For every cube there are a corner permutation #{\sigma\in S_8} and corner
orientation vector #{x\in(\mathbb Z_3)^8}. For odd #{N}, there is also a
single-edge permutation #{\tau_s\in S_{12}} and flip vector
#{z\in(\mathbb Z_2)^{12}}. Each coupled-edge orbit indexed by
#{i\in I} has a permutation #{\tau_i\in S_{24}} and a labelled orientation
vector #{y_i\in(\mathbb Z_2)^{24}}. Each center-corner orbit has a
permutation #{\rho_i\in S_{24}}. Center-edge orbits are indexed by a finite
set #{J}; write #{\rho_{ij}} for the orbit whose two distance signatures are
#{i,j}. In the fixed-center convention, #{I=\{1,\ldots,K_o\}} and
#{J=I^2} when #{N} is odd, while #{I=\{1,\ldots,K_e\}} and
#{J=\{(i,j)\in I^2:i\ne j\}} when #{N} is even. This indexing records
which coupled-edge signatures border a center-edge orbit; it is the
coordinate version of the rings in the preceding chapter.}

  \p{The full labelled configuration space is the product of these factors,
with the solved state represented by identity permutations and zero
orientation vectors. It is deliberately larger than the move group. For
example, a vector #{y_i} records all sticker orientations allowed by a
labelled reassembly, even though the legal slice action fixes the relevant
coupled-edge orientation in its orbit. Whole-cube rotations are still outside
the generator set, and the six face directions remain part of the frame.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{The invariant law}

  \p{For odd #{N}, a configuration is legal exactly when}
  ##{\begin{aligned}
  \operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)&=\operatorname{sgn}(\tau_s)=
       \operatorname{sgn}(\rho_i) &&(i\in I),\\
  \operatorname{sgn}(\rho_{ij})&=\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)
       \operatorname{sgn}(\tau_i)\operatorname{sgn}(\tau_j) &&((i,j)\in J),\\
  \sum_r x_r&=0\pmod 3,\qquad \sum_r z_r=0\pmod 2,\\
  y_i&=0 &&(i\in I).
  \end{aligned}}
  \p{For even #{N}, the corresponding law is}
  ##{\begin{aligned}
  \operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)&=\operatorname{sgn}(\rho_i) &&(i\in I),\\
  \operatorname{sgn}(\rho_{ij})&=\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)
       \operatorname{sgn}(\tau_i)\operatorname{sgn}(\tau_j) &&((i,j)\in J),\\
  \sum_r x_r&=0\pmod 3,\\
  y_i&=0 &&(i\in I).
  \end{aligned}}
  \p{The empty-index cases are part of the statement: #{N=2} has only
corners, #{N=3} has the usual twelve single edges and fixed centers, and
#{N=4} has one coupled-edge and one center-corner orbit but no center-edge
orbit. The #{N=5} instance has one orbit of each coupled-edge, center-corner
and center-edge type, giving the 98 visible cubies counted earlier.}

  \p{Necessity follows by checking generators. An outer face turn is a
4-cycle on corners, single edges, center corners and each relevant
center-edge orbit, and two 4-cycles on each affected coupled-edge orbit.
An inner slice turn is the identity on corners and single edges, and is a
4-cycle on its coupled edges and on the matching center-edge orbits, with
two 4-cycles on center corners. These cycle signs give the two parity rows.
The corner character changes by two opposite units on a non-#{U}/#{D}
face and not at all on a #{U}/#{D} face, so its sum is fixed modulo 3. An
outer turn flips four single edges, so their sum is fixed modulo 2. The
chosen coupled-edge sticker convention makes every legal #{y_i} zero. The
solved state satisfies the rows, hence every word in the generators does.}

  \p{Bonzio--Loi--Peruzzi give the labelled Professor's-Cube version of the
same tuple and its corrected coupled-edge condition
\citet{Theorem 5}{bonzio2018nxnxn}. Salkinder states the odd and even
families and checks these generator effects in
\citet{Theorems 8--9}{salkinder2021nxnxn}. Their notation indexes center-edge
orbits by the two adjacent coupled-edge signatures; our #{J} makes that
indexing explicit. The sources also use a shifted notation for a middle
slice when constructing a single-edge 3-cycle. That auxiliary word is a
composition of parallel slice turns in the fixed-center model; it does not
add a central generator to the law above.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Why the conditions are sufficient}

  \p{The converse uses localized effects rather than a counting guess. First
use a face turn, or an internal slice turn, to correct the common parity
signs. The sign equations ensure that after this step every permutation
factor is even. Commutators and conjugates then supply the required local
subgroups:}
  \ul{
    \li{a corner 3-cycle, for example
      #{[[R:U],D]}, generates #{A_8} on corners;}
    \li{the single-edge 3-cycle #{[F,[R:S]]} generates #{A_{12}} in the odd
      case, with the middle-slice symbol interpreted by the parallel-slice
      composition just described;}
    \li{#{[[R_j^{-1},D_i],F^{-1}]} gives 3-cycles on a center-corner orbit
      when #{i=j} and on a center-edge orbit when #{i\ne j};}
    \li{the analogous coupled-edge commutator gives #{A_{24}} on each
      coupled-edge orbit. In even size the source's explicit odd permutation
      on that orbit upgrades it to #{S_{24}}; in odd size the parity row keeps
      it at #{A_{24}}.}
  }
  \p{Conjugating these words moves each 3-cycle through its whole orbit.
Thus the local subgroups clear the corner, single-edge, center-corner,
center-edge and coupled-edge permutations in turn. Finally, commutators
that twist two corners in opposite directions generate the kernel of the
corner-sum map #{(\mathbb Z_3)^8\to\mathbb Z_3}; the corresponding
edge-flip commutators generate the kernel of the single-edge sum. The
coupled-edge orientation vector is already zero by its law. The parity and
orientation equations are exactly what is needed for each cleanup step, so
reversing the resulting word reaches every tuple satisfying the displayed
conditions. This is the constructive sufficiency argument of
\citet{Theorems 10--18}{salkinder2021nxnxn}; it is a subgroup generation
argument, not an assertion that one universal short algorithm solves every
size.}

  \p{There are two boundaries to keep visible. The theorem concerns labelled
positions and a fixed frame; a colour-only observation quotients by equal
labels and may identify several legal labelled states. Also, Bonzio's and
Salkinder's orbit labels are source conventions, so a numerical state count
must be transported through the index sets before comparison. The next
chapter specializes the same action to #{N=3}, where all center and
coupled-edge factors disappear and the three familiar constraints can be
worked through with explicit face generators.}
}
